   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">External review of the WSRC through targeted interviews and a SWOT analysis on the basis of which clear goals can be set and weaknesses shown (to be conducted at the end of the project).
 Trainings carried out on basis of staff needs assessment and a training Plan 2022-2025.
50% of staff trained.

Implement MoU between WSRC and PWA, 12 meetings for the 6 working committees are held and documented.
(at least 2 official meeting for each committee)

Existing plan aiming at financial sustainability within 10 years updated and implemented; 10% of council required funding is secured from licensing fees.
 Revenues generated via license fees increased (40,000 USD is the program year one target).</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A financial external review/audit is made annually. In the first program year this was not realized because this was not a requirement.
Assessments were made during former years but never properly documented. This, however, is not a proper structural way of developing the capacities of the staff of the WSRC. 
A number of training programs were completed and reported. The trainings included: Excel, benchmarking, wastewater treatment, M&amp;E, etc. During the first program year, fifty percent of the WSRC staff were trained in one or more of these topics. This has included 4 staff members out of 8 at that time.
All planned meetings were completed,
During program year 1, several issues within the MoU could not be completed by the council especially the article related to licensing for the above-mentioned reasons, the completion of the incentives program, again for limited clarity at that time and the council financial sustainability which is directly linked to licensing.
No cash has been received yet during the first program year. The reason for this is the council was not able to collect fees before the completion of the legal instruments, i.e RWUs instructions, and that last year, SPs were not prepared to pay the 1000 NIS as it was not budgeted.
this was not realized for the above-mentioned reasons.</narrative>
    </description>
